# RockboxPhoto

> Custom Rockbox firmware for the fourth-generation iPod Photo/Color.
> Native 220x176 UI, dynamic colors, iFlash reliability, and lower overhead.

RockboxPhoto develops directly on official Rockbox history while focusing on
the 32 MB iPod Photo, initially the A1099. It keeps the full Rockbox playback
experience and makes the interface, storage path, power behavior, and recovery
logic fit this hardware rather than treating it as a scaled-down later iPod.

## Highlights

### A native Photo interface

- Purpose-built 220x176 menu, browser, status bar, and now-playing layouts with
  Photo-sized fonts, icons, spacing, and selectors.
- Cover Flow is a first-class main-menu destination. Album art uses a rounded,
  color-aware mask in both the menu and now-playing card.
- Battery graphics distinguish discharging, charging, and fully charged states.
  Large fonts, short viewports, list pagination, and click-wheel edge cases are
  handled without sacrificing the Photo's limited screen space.

### Dynamic album-art colors

- Dominant and accent colors are extracted when artwork changes and cached;
  the UI does not rebuild a histogram on every frame.
- Foreground, background, selector, separator, muted-surface, status-bar, and
  now-playing colors follow the current album with a 250 ms transition.
- Contrast is enforced, missing artwork falls back to theme colors, and the
  feature can be changed under Theme Settings. It is enabled by default on the
  iPod Photo.

### iFlash-ready storage

- Brings up the installed ATA1 iFlash adapter and SD card for normal boot,
  database generation, playback, and USB mass storage.
- Retains conservative Apple PIO timing and the Photo's UDMA2 ceiling while
  adding responsive DMA completion, IDE-interrupt waits, bounded timeouts, and
  automatic per-transfer PIO recovery.
- Uses exact idle, retry, wake, and delayed-power-off deadlines and flushes
  durable data before shutdown, USB cache sync, eject, and disconnect.

### Faster library and artwork workflows

- Buffers database records and indexes, batches updates, reuses metadata work,
  and reduces database-ready polling.
- Publishes database and persistent state atomically, recovers interrupted
  commits, and rejects malformed records, paths, filesystem structures, media
  metadata, skins, and artwork before unsafe access.
- PictureFlow caching is paced and failure-safe, skips unchanged idle redraws,
  and presents an immediate placeholder when artwork is missing or corrupt.

### Better power and USB behavior

- Reduces idle PMU, backlight, database, and storage-thread wakeups while
  preserving responsive accessory and input handling.
- Supports realistic replacement-battery capacities and clean shutdown while
  charging or using charge-only USB without immediately waking again.
- Shows **Safe to disconnect** after the host ejects every exposed disk and
  uses a clean reboot to apply firmware copied over USB when the PP5020's
  post-USB ROLO handoff would be unreliable.

### Reliability with useful telemetry

- Hardens iAP, USB mass storage, ATA, FAT/GPT, codecs, playlists, database
  navigation, PictureFlow, skins, click-wheel input, and persistent files
  against invalid lengths, indices, partial writes, and failed recovery.
- Keeps performance telemetry in RAM: cache maintenance, DMA latency, IRQ
  quality, PIO recovery, wake sources, and PCM transition production can be
  captured as a one-shot snapshot without continuous SD-card logging.

## Hardware status

Conservative firmware has booted successfully on an installed A1099. A
deterministic 256 MiB USB integrity pass and the first measured playback
transition pass completed without DMA timeouts, PIO fallbacks, IRQ anomalies,
recovery failures, or audio underruns. Broader power, accessory, theme, and
full-lifecycle qualification remains in progress.

See the [iPod Photo roadmap](../IPOD_PHOTO_ROADMAP.md) for the complete
implementation checklist, measurements, and remaining hardware work.

## Build from source

The validated Photo builds use the Rockbox GCC 9.5.0 ARM toolchain. It can be
generated with **tools/rockboxdev.sh**; Perl is also required.

~~~sh
git clone https://github.com/jspann21/rockbox-ipod-photo.git
cd rockbox-ipod-photo
mkdir build-ipodcolor
cd build-ipodcolor
../tools/configure
~~~

Select the **iPod Color/Photo** target and a normal firmware build, then build
and package it:

~~~sh
make
make zip
~~~

Extract **rockbox.zip** to an iPod that already has the official Rockbox
bootloader installed, safely eject it, and reboot.

